1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,446
Salaam. This is Liam, in for Bear. Claude Code has an effort dial, and it's tempting

2
00:00:04,466 --> 00:00:08,911
to always turn it up. The better question is how to match effort to the job.

3
00:00:08,981 --> 00:00:13,899
Three terms. Effort: how much compute Claude spends on a task. An edge case: an

4
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:18,508
unusual input where code tends to break. And slash effort: the Claude Code command

5
00:00:18,528 --> 00:00:20,154
that changes the level mid-conversation.

6
00:00:20,224 --> 00:00:25,627
Here's the dial. Five settings: low, medium, high, x high, and max. Opus five point

7
00:00:25,647 --> 00:00:29,242
five starts at medium. Most other models start at high.

8
00:00:29,312 --> 00:00:33,479
So what is effort, really? Thariq Shihipar, in his post Spending your effort, says

9
00:00:33,499 --> 00:00:38,564
it gives the model an approximation of how much compute you want it to spend on the

10
00:00:38,584 --> 00:00:38,863
task.

11
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:43,513
Think of it like a deadline. Given one hour, you'd bring the best version that meets

12
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:47,250
the task, then expect to iterate. Given twelve hours straight, you'd assume they

13
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:48,975
want you to try very hard.

14
00:00:49,045 --> 00:00:54,148
Effort works the same way. Claude always tries to do your task reasonably, but

15
00:00:54,168 --> 00:00:57,807
higher effort means more independent action on verification and judgement.

16
00:00:57,877 --> 00:01:03,229
The clearest case is HTML JS filter, a Terminal-Bench three point oh task: an HTML

17
00:01:03,249 --> 00:01:07,886
sanitizer that strips every way of smuggling JavaScript into a page. In Anthropic's

18
00:01:07,906 --> 00:01:13,616
runs, Fable five point one on low passed one of five tries: about two minutes, one

19
00:01:13,636 --> 00:01:15,407
pass, one hand-written test page.

20
00:01:15,477 --> 00:01:20,350
On x high, it passed five of five. The run he traced took about thirty-three

21
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:23,939
minutes. It reviewed its own draft adversarially, read the installed parser's

22
00:01:23,959 --> 00:01:28,506
source, ran a standard X S S test suite, and wrote a random-document fuzzer.

23
00:01:28,576 --> 00:01:33,779
His rule of thumb. Low for quick responses while you're in the loop: brainstorming,

24
00:01:33,799 --> 00:01:37,882
sketching, easy changes. Medium for most regular engineering work, like new

25
00:01:37,902 --> 00:01:38,255
features.

26
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:43,445
High where verification matters or there are edge cases, like fixing a bug. Max when

27
00:01:43,465 --> 00:01:47,215
you want Claude working fully on its own on difficult problems.

28
00:01:47,285 --> 00:01:52,474
And his loop for feature work. Have Claude interview you about the spec. Build it on

29
00:01:52,494 --> 00:01:57,358
low. Review it, iterating on low. Then verify and test on high. In Claude Code,

30
00:01:57,378 --> 00:01:59,311
slash effort switches the level mid-conversation.

31
00:01:59,381 --> 00:02:03,332
Your turn. Paste this into Claude Code: Plan one real task with me through the

32
00:02:03,352 --> 00:02:07,039
effort loop: name your task. First, interview me about any details I'm missing. Then

33
00:02:07,059 --> 00:02:11,010
list which steps to run on low effort and which on high. For the high-effort

34
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:14,452
verification, say exactly what it must check: which edge cases, which tests, and

35
00:02:14,472 --> 00:02:18,159
what counts as a failure. Don't build anything yet. Then check two things yourself.

36
00:02:18,179 --> 00:02:21,865
Does every high step name something concrete to verify? And build on low, then

37
00:02:21,885 --> 00:02:25,572
switch to high with slash effort to verify. Did it catch what low missed?

38
00:02:25,642 --> 00:02:29,452
Spending Your Effort. At Nik Bear Brown.
